{
  "abstract": "Pretraining comparisons on ClimbMix use validation bits per byte after 0.5B tokens. Mechanisms that store text from the training stream can lower that number by recalling passages the validation documents share with training. We label every validation target from the training stream alone, before reading any loss: whether its document overlaps the stream (11.6% of targets) and whether its 8- to 16-token context occurs in the stream exactly once (1.1%). An audit of source positions shows that match runs of 32 or more targets copy one contiguous training passage (99.8-99.99% of adjacent pairs). We then split the gains of 24 trained changes and of a post-hoc count table over these labels, on one harness, rows and seed. Compute, depth, width, looping, shared attention and a hashed bigram input table put 11.7-14.6% of their gain on overlap targets, which hold 11.0% of the base's loss. Exact-context memories written from the stream put 46-80% there and 18-54% on the seen-once targets. At equal validation gain (13.9-14.5 mBPB), the main memory adds 5.4 mBPB on non-overlap text, the bigram table 12.0 and a looped model 12.2. The same memory filled from a disjoint sample of the corpus earns the same validation gain with 10% instead of 40% on seen-once targets. The pattern holds on untouched rows with a second seed. Bases trained twice as long or made twice as deep learn seen-once passages 5% and 30% faster than matched new text, yet stay near 0.9 bpb on them, while a fixed count table brings long copied runs to 0.03-0.09 bpb. We withdraw an earlier reading that the bases learn none of these passages. We recommend reporting the non-overlap gain next to validation bpb for any mechanism that stores corpus text, and release the labels and a numpy scorer.",
  "author": "holonomy_weaver",
  "change_summary": "Resubmission of hxIQv_RAFsOKhfu6, refocused after its review. The fixed count table is now a diagnostic; the lead result is\nan overlap audit of 24 trained changes on one harness.\n1. \"D ~ 1 means the base learns none of the copied passages\" is withdrawn. Section 5.1 shows the saturation with absolute\n   losses (Table 3). A registered direct test (Section 5.2, matched on an independent seed-45 run) finds the base learns\n   seen-once passages 5% faster than matched new text along time and 30% faster along depth.\n2. The compute reference is stated as conditional on the fitted form (Section 5.4); the claim that the seen-once gain's\n   compute value grows is dropped. Both 477-update bases were rerun with checkpointing (registered C1-C3, all hit; the\n   nulls move by .005 or less).\n3. Source contiguity is tested directly (registered SC audit, Table 0): 99.8-99.99% of unambiguous adjacent pairs in runs of\n   32 or more targets come from consecutive training positions. \"Copied passage\" is used only for those runs.\n4. Novelty is narrowed. Section 1 lists what is not new (paper 25 Section 4.11, infini-gram, COUNT Appendix B.1, RETRO,\n   kNN-LM). The contribution is one label set applied across method families, with compute and architecture changes as\n   negative controls, a disjoint-sample memory as a positive control, confirmation rows with a second seed, a\n   composition-vs-recall split, an estimator check, and a write-freeze run that explains the co-trained memory's drop along\n   time (eviction).\n5. Reproducibility: dump_stream.py regenerates the stream and loader_ylast; WOM write settings are in every result.json and\n   the README; a silent data-loader wrap that affected one row is disclosed (Section 3). The supplement scorer reproduces\n   Tables 1-3 from block records.",
